 "drivingQuestion": "What counts as a quarrel between husband and wife?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Quarrel redefined as her claim that he divorced her; pressed further in the next unit.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yevamot.116a.8",
   "daf": "116a",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "קְטָטָה בֵּינוֹ לְבֵינָהּ וְכוּ׳. הֵיכִי דָּמֵי קְטָטָה בֵּינוֹ לְבֵינָהּ, אָמַר רַב יְהוּדָה אָמַר שְׁמוּאֵל: בְּאוֹמֶרֶת לְבַעְלָהּ ״גָּרְשֵׁינִי״. כּוּלְּהוּ נָמֵי אָמְרוּ הָכִי! אֶלָּא בְּאוֹמֶרֶת לְבַעְלָהּ ״גֵּירַשְׁתַּנִי״.",
   "en": "§ The mishna taught: If there was «a quarrel between him and her,» her testimony that her husband died is not accepted. The Gemara asks: «What are the circumstances» of a «quarrel between him and her? Rav Yehuda said» that «Shmuel said:» This is a case «where» people heard «her say to her husband: Divorce me.» The Gemara asks: Is this proof? «All» women «likewise say this» when they are angry; this does not prove that there was an unresolved quarrel left between them. «Rather,» a quarrel is «when she says to her husband: You divorced me,» i.e., she claims that she was actually divorced."
  }
 ],
